---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VcardQrCodeGenerator from '@/components/tools/generators/VcardQrCodeGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="vcard-qr-code-generator">
  <VcardQrCodeGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to make a contact QR code</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Fill in the fields you want on the card. The tool leaves blank fields out of the vCard; it does not write empty properties.</li>
      <li>Watch the byte counter under the code. Below about 300 bytes, the code scans easily from a printed business card; above 400 the symbol gets dense.</li>
      <li>Keep the error-correction level at L or M. A vCard is already a lot of data, and extra redundancy makes the symbol larger with no benefit on a clean printed card.</li>
      <li>Download the SVG for print, the PNG for a screen, or the <code>.vcf</code> file to email the contact.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What a vCard contains</h2>
    <p>
      vCard is a plain-text format defined by RFC 2426 (version 3.0) and RFC 6350 (version 4.0). This tool
      writes 3.0, because iOS, Android, Outlook and Google Contacts all read it reliably from a QR code. A
      finished card looks like this:
    </p>
    <pre><code>BEGIN:VCARD
VERSION:3.0
N:Lovelace;Ada;;;
FN:Ada Lovelace
ORG:Analytical Engines Ltd
TITLE:Head of Computation
TEL;TYPE=CELL,VOICE:+44 20 7946 0958
EMAIL;TYPE=INTERNET,PREF:ada@example.com
URL:https://example.com
ADR;TYPE=WORK:;;12 Marylebone Road;London;;NW1 5LS;United Kingdom
END:VCARD</code></pre>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Property</th>
            <th>Holds</th>
            <th>Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>Family;Given;Additional;Prefix;Suffix</td><td>Structured name; the five parts are always present, even when empty</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>FN</code></td><td>Formatted name</td><td>The only mandatory display field; what the phone shows</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ORG</code></td><td>Organisation</td><td>Sub-units are separated with semicolons</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>TEL</code></td><td>Phone number</td><td><code>TYPE=CELL</code>, <code>WORK</code>, <code>HOME</code>, <code>FAX</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>EMAIL</code></td><td>Address</td><td><code>PREF</code> marks the preferred one</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ADR</code></td><td>PO box;Extended;Street;City;Region;Postcode;Country</td><td>Seven parts, the first two normally left empty</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>URL</code></td><td>Website</td><td>Include the scheme or some clients will not linkify it</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>NOTE</code></td><td>Free text</td><td>Newlines are written as <code>\n</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Inside a value, backslash, semicolon and comma are reserved, and the tool escapes them. Line breaks become
      <code>\n</code>. So a company name such as “Smith, Jones &amp; Co.” does not split into two fields.
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping the vCard QR code scannable</h2>
    <p>
      Density is the main problem with contact QR codes. Each character of vCard text adds modules to the grid,
      and at some point a phone camera can no longer resolve them at business-card size. Ways to cut bytes:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>One phone number, not three. Mobile is almost always the one people want.</li>
      <li>Leave out the address unless people visit you. It is usually 60–90 bytes on its own.</li>
      <li>Leave out the note. People who scan your card seldom read it.</li>
      <li>Shorten the URL. <code>example.com</code> works as well as <code>https://www.example.com/team/ada-lovelace</code> if the short form redirects.</li>
      <li>Use level L or M correction. A clean, flat printed card needs little correction.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>vCard code or a link to a profile page?</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>vCard QR code</th>
            <th>Link to a page</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Works offline</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Saves in one tap</td><td>Yes</td><td>Usually two or three</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Details can be changed later</td><td>No: reprint required</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Can show a photo or logo</td><td>Not practically</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tells you who scanned</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, if you add analytics</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Depends on a service staying online</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      On a conference badge or a printed card that will outlast a job title, many people put both: a vCard code
      on the back for instant saving, and a short URL in text for details that change.
    </p>

    <h2>Testing before you print</h2>
    <p>
      Print one card at final size on the real card stock, then scan it with an iPhone camera, an Android camera
      and one third-party scanner, at arm’s length, in normal office light. Check that the name, phone and email
      arrive correctly, most of all accented characters and long organisation names. Then order the print run.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
